CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member operator<< found via a reference-typed operand: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
N5008 [over.match.oper]/3.2: the member candidate set for `a @ b` is the
qualified lookup of `T1::operator@`, where T1 is the type of the left operand.
An lvalue of reference-to-class type -- e.g. `static_cast<std::ostream&>(x)`,
which CBMC represents with a reference type rather than a bare struct_tag --
is an lvalue of the referenced class type and so has that class's member
operators as candidates.  Here `static_cast<ostream&>(base) << 42` inside a
member operator<< template body must select ostream::operator<<(int).
Regression for the dog-food src/util (parse_options.cpp, typecheck.cpp) shl
noise.  assertion.2 ("WRONG") must FAIL (non-vacuity).
